@meta
id: 25-15
title: When :normal needs :execute
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: ex-normal
par: 45
minutes: 7
vscode: type quotes and commas on six lines by hand
open: tags.ts

@brief
Six bare tag names inside an array that expects string literals. Each one
becomes an indented, quoted, comma-terminated element:

    blue-mug        →       'blue-mug',

On one line that is `I  '`, Esc, `A',`. The Esc is in the middle, and
that is the problem. Everything after `:normal ` is taken literally, so
typing an actual Esc key there ends the command line instead of going
into the file. `:normal` supplies an Esc at the *end* of each line and
nowhere else.

The fix is to build the string in an expression, where `\<Esc>` is
ordinary text that vim turns into the key. `:execute` evaluates the
expression and runs the result as a command line.

@keys
| `:execute "2,7normal I  '\<Esc>A',"` | run those keys, Esc and all, on lines 2 to 7 |
| `\<Esc>` `\<CR>` `\<C-a>` | the key notation, inside a double-quoted string |
| `:execute` | evaluate a string, then run it as an Ex command |

@hints
Write the keys for a single line first, with Esc where you would press
it. Then the whole thing has to become a string.
--
The range goes inside the string, in front of `normal` — `:execute` does
not take one of its own.
--
`:execute "2,7normal I  '\<Esc>A',"` Enter, then `:wq`. Double quotes,
not single: `\<Esc>` is only interpreted inside double quotes.

@solution
    :execute "2,7normal I  '<Esc>A',"<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

written out in full as

    :execute "2,7normal I  '\<Esc>A',"

Thirty-nine keystrokes. It is the longest command in the chapter so far
and there is no shorter honest version, because a real Esc cannot be
typed into a command line as data.

The rule is small: **`:normal` cannot contain a special key, `:execute`
can.** Esc, Enter, Ctrl-A, Ctrl-V — anything with a name in angle
brackets — has to go through `:execute` and a double-quoted string.
Single quotes will not do it; vim only interprets `\<...>` inside double
quotes.

The most common case is Enter rather than Esc:

    :execute "normal! ci(\<CR>"          replace an argument list with a newline
    :execute "%normal! A\<C-a>"          increment the last number on every line
    :execute "'a,'bnormal! @q\<CR>"      rarely needed — @q ends cleanly on its own

And a shape worth stealing, since `:execute` builds a command line out of
any string:

    :execute "normal! " . repeat("x", 12)

`:normal` on its own is right whenever the keys are all printable, which
is most of the time — `A;`, `I// `, `@q`, `dw`. Reach for `:execute` the
moment one of them is not.

@start tags.ts
export const tags = [
blue-mug
grey-mug
enamel-pin
canvas-tote
sticker-pack
desk-mat
];

@expected tags.ts
export const tags = [
  'blue-mug',
  'grey-mug',
  'enamel-pin',
  'canvas-tote',
  'sticker-pack',
  'desk-mat',
];
